; A trait declares one value name per method, so a top-level `fn` of
; that spelling is a duplicate definition - the same answer `macro`
; has given since hygiene was designed, and the same answer for the
; same reason: the two do not coexist, one of them silently wins
; every call. Before this, `(trait (Sz a) where (sz :: (-> a Int)))`
; beside `(fn (sz n) 7)` checked OK and answered 111.
;
; Both orders are here because `firstDefiner` scans backwards from
; the declaration it is reporting: `sz` finds the trait behind it,
; and the trait `Tw` finds the function behind it through a different
; arm. One arm working and the other silent is the shape that hid
; this in the first place.
(trait (Sz a) where
  (sz :: (-> a Int)))

(fn (sz n) 7)

(fn (tw n) 1)

(trait (Tw a) where
  (tw :: (-> a Int)))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
